---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Disclosure from '~/components/Disclosure.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/JsonLd.astro';
import PageHeader from '~/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Section from '~/components/Section.astro';
import VacancyCalculator from '~/components/VacancyCalculator.astro';

import { breadcrumbs } from '~/lib/schema';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Owner Tools"
  description="A vacancy cost calculator for Northern Virginia rental owners: what an empty property costs, and how many extra vacant days a higher rent can justify. Free to use, no sign-up."
>
  <JsonLd slot="head" schema={breadcrumbs([{ name: 'Owner Tools', path: '/tools' }])} />

  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Owner tools"
    title="Vacancy Cost Calculator"
    lead="An empty property costs money every day it sits. This works out how much, and whether holding out for a higher rent is worth the wait. Free to use, and nothing is sent anywhere."
  />

  <Section tone="sage">
    <VacancyCalculator />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-16">
      <div class="prose-site">
        <h2 class="text-h2">How the numbers work</h2>

        <h3>Vacancy cost</h3>
        <p>
          Rent and carrying costs are converted to a daily figure using an
          average month of 30.4 days, then multiplied by the days vacant.
          Turnover expenses are added once, not per day.
        </p>
        <p>
          The carrying costs are shown separately on purpose. A mortgage
          payment, HOA fee and insurance premium are owed whether or not
          anyone is living there, so they are not caused by the vacancy. What
          the vacancy does is remove the rent that would otherwise meet them,
          which is why the total is described as cash-flow exposure rather
          than a loss.
        </p>

        <h3>Break-even on a higher rent</h3>
        <p>
          This compares two outcomes over the same lease term, so the
          comparison is like for like: letting now at your asking rent, or
          letting later at the higher rent after some additional empty days.
          The break-even is the point where both leave you with the same
          rental income over that period.
        </p>
        <p>
          Carrying costs are deliberately left out of this second calculation.
          They are identical in both scenarios, so including them would
          understate how long you can afford to wait. The figure does account
          for something owners often miss: days spent waiting are days that no
          longer earn rent inside the same fixed term.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assumption is that the only cost of waiting is the rent you do
          not collect. If a longer marketing period means real extra spend,
          that is not modelled here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <Disclosure label="Please read">
          <p>
            This calculator provides general estimates for educational
            purposes only. Results are based on user-provided assumptions and
            do not constitute financial, tax, legal, or investment advice.
            Actual rental performance and vacancy periods may vary.
          </p>
        </Disclosure>

        <div class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <h2 class="font-serif text-base font-semibold text-ink">
            Your figures stay with you
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            Everything you type here is worked out in your own browser.
            Nothing is sent to us, stored, or required before you see your
            results. If you choose to request a rental evaluation, the figures
            are copied into the message box on our contact form so you can
            check them before anything is sent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
